## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a candidate’s ability to apply parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR) across multiple lanes in a 128-bit value, without loops, while extracting and packing results into specific lanes. It also probes attention to data layout and endianness in lane-oriented operations.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) for 32-bit lanes
- Bit masks and shifts to accumulate per-lane popcounts
- 128-bit data viewed as four 32-bit lanes and proper packing of results

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 perLanePopcount128(uint128 x) {
  auto count32 = [](uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0xFFULL;
  };

  uint64_t loCount  = count32(x.lo);
  uint64_t hiCount  = count32(x.hi);

  uint128 r = {0, 0};
  // lane0: lo low 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo
  r.lo = (loCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane1: lo high 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo at bits 32-39
  r.lo |= (loCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  // lane2: hi low 32 bits -> bits 64-71 of r.hi
  r.hi = (hiCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane3: hi high 32 bits -> bits 96-103 of r.hi
  r.hi |= (hiCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary lane widths (e.g., 64-bit lanes) while preserving parallelism?
- What are the potential pitfalls with endianness or portability across compilers for the approach above?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive circular adjacency via rotation, and to implement a 128-bit popcount using SWAR style reductions; ensures understanding of cross-boundary bit relationships.

## Key Concepts

- Circular adjacency using ROTL
- 128-bit representation as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR/popcount with bitwise masks and shifts

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint128 rotl128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  return r;
}

static inline unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int ca = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int cb = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return ca + cb;
}
```

